WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:11.760
There are many great Gothic cathedrals in Europe,&nbsp;
but few produce the same feeling as Chartres.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.800
When you step inside, the air itself seems blue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.040
light pours through the stained glass&nbsp;
and fills the entire building with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:29.360
deep reds and violet-blue tones, creating an&nbsp;
atmosphere unlike that of any other church.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:35.680
It’s easy to think that this effect is simply&nbsp;
the result of extraordinary stained glass.

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:39.600
But the astonishing windows of&nbsp;
Chartres are only possible because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:46.160
of a series of structural innovations that&nbsp;
transformed Gothic architecture forever.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.800
Two factors make Chartres Cathedral&nbsp;
one of the most luminous, colorful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.920
and powerful medieval churches in Europe.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.000
One is indeed its stained glass,&nbsp;
which we will return to later.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.400
The other is the architecture itself:&nbsp;
the structural innovation of the Gothic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:06.240
system developed in Chartres&nbsp;
that allowed for larger spaces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:13.200
larger openings, and ultimately more light.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.520
Architecturally, Chartres Cathedral differs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.200
substantially from earlier&nbsp;
French Gothic cathedrals.

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.280
The architects at Chartres needed to&nbsp;
find better ways of dematerializing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.680
the structure in order to accommodate&nbsp;
an ambitious architectural program.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:32.960
Chartres was one of the great pilgrimage&nbsp;
destinations of medieval Europe.

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.760
People from all over northern Europe traveled to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:42.000
venerate the most precious relic of&nbsp;
Chartres: the tunic of Virgin Mary.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.640
This relic was said to have been brought&nbsp;
from the Holy Land to Constantinople in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:52.480
the 8th century, before being gifted to&nbsp;
Charlemagne in the early 9th century.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:59.120
In 876, Charlemagne’s grandson, King Charles&nbsp;
the Bald, donated the relic to Chartres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:05.680
where it quickly became one of the most important&nbsp;
objects of pilgrimage in medieval France.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:10.480
The present cathedral was built between&nbsp;
the late 12th and early 13th centuries.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.120
The architects had to design a building&nbsp;
capable not only of housing the relic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:23.520
but also of accommodating the immense&nbsp;
number of pilgrims who came to see it.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.840
The differences become apparent when we compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:31.360
Chartres with earlier churches such as&nbsp;
Laon Cathedral, or Notre Dame in Paris.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:38.880
In these earlier buildings, we find narrower&nbsp;
interior spaces, lower vaults, and thicker walls.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:45.840
The nave arcades are supported by small,&nbsp;
cylindrical columns placed close together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.880
from which alternating shafts&nbsp;
rise to support sexpartite vaults.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:58.080
At Chartres, instead of small columns&nbsp;
we see large piers composed of clustered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:06.320
shafts that rise uninterrupted from the&nbsp;
floor to the springing of the vaults.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.120
These piers support quadripartite vaults,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.720
allowing each bay to span a greater&nbsp;
distance and reach a greater height.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:19.280
The result is not only a broader&nbsp;
and more unified interior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:25.760
but also a significant increase in&nbsp;
the size of the clerestory windows.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.240
Instead of the single lancet windows&nbsp;
found in many earlier churches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:40.320
each bay at Chartres contains a pair of&nbsp;
lancets surmounted by a small rose window.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.760
The flying buttresses were equally important.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:52.240
Unlike earlier cathedrals, where buttresses were&nbsp;
often added or modified during construction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:59.200
the architects of Chartres planned them from&nbsp;
the beginning as an integral part of the design.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.560
Their system of triple arched&nbsp;
flying buttresses transferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.760
the thrust efficiently to the exterior,&nbsp;
allowing to eliminate the tribune gallery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:11.520
freeing even more wall surface&nbsp;
for a larger clerestory.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:17.360
As a result, the traditional four-level&nbsp;
elevation was simplified into three levels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.960
creating a lighter and more open interior.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:24.880
All these structural innovations not&nbsp;
only increased the amount of light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:29.280
entering the building, they also&nbsp;
created spaces that feel wider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:36.400
taller, and more spacious than&nbsp;
those of earlier cathedrals.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.600
Radiating chapels around the&nbsp;
ambulatory, behind the altar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.560
were already a common feature in churches&nbsp;
since the early Middle Ages—they were used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:50.960
to house relics of saints and martyrs, and&nbsp;
pilgrims would often visit a specific chapel.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:59.040
Early Gothic churches in France such as&nbsp;
Saint Denis, have relatively shallow chapels.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:04.720
Because Chartres possessed one of the most&nbsp;
important relics in Europe, its architects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:12.560
expanded this arrangement, alternating three&nbsp;
shallow chapels with three much deeper ones.

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:17.600
The tunic of the Virgin was housed&nbsp;
in one of these larger chapels.

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:22.480
The circulation system was also&nbsp;
redesigned to accommodate large crowds.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:28.640
The ambulatory and choir were enlarged&nbsp;
into a continuous double-aisled corridor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:34.080
rather than the single ambulatory&nbsp;
found in many earlier cathedrals.

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:40.720
Vast aisled transepts with triple portals on the&nbsp;
north and south sides further increased access,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.400
allowing pilgrims to move through&nbsp;
the church more efficiently.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:49.920
With these programmatic changes—the&nbsp;
double ambulatory, enlarged transepts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.160
and expanded pilgrimage spaces—and with&nbsp;
the rationalization of the structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.160 --> 00:06:01.040
into clustered piers, quadripartite&nbsp;
vaults, and a three-level elevation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:06.480
Chartres Cathedral marks the transition&nbsp;
from Early Gothic to High Gothic.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:13.120
Later cathedrals would adopt the structural&nbsp;
logic established here and push it even further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:22.080
creating buildings that were taller,&nbsp;
more slender, and even more luminous.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:27.120
For medieval thinkers, light was&nbsp;
not merely a practical necessity.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:31.040
It was a manifestation of the&nbsp;
presence and the power of God.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:35.520
But the sacred light at Chartres&nbsp;
is not just about the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:41.840
light that enters the building, but&nbsp;
also about the quality of that light.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:45.360
Beyond the vast spaces and enlarged openings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:53.360
the other element that gives Chartres Cathedral&nbsp;
its extraordinary luminosity is its stained glass.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:07:00.800
An exceptional amount of 12th and 13th century&nbsp;
stained glass survives at Chartres, making it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:08.160
the most complete and best-preserved&nbsp;
medieval glazing ensemble in Europe.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:12.560
Stepping inside the cathedral immediately&nbsp;
transports you to another age,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:16.720
because there’s something about these&nbsp;
windows that makes them so colorful that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:22.880
the very air inside the cathedral seems&nbsp;
to be tinted in this blue-violet tone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:32.800
and the atmosphere that you perceive is&nbsp;
one of peace, mystery, and transcendence.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.880
All of the windows contribute to the blue&nbsp;
atmosphere perceived inside the cathedral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:39.760
but it’s especially the three lancet windows above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:44.800
the western entrance that seem&nbsp;
to bathe the interior in color.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:52.080
These windows are among the few surviving&nbsp;
examples of 12th century stained glass.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.880
Most medieval glass that survives in&nbsp;
Europe dates from the 13th century or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.520
later, when glazing techniques had&nbsp;
become increasingly sophisticated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:08.560
and artists were capable of producing more&nbsp;
refined figures and more complex compositions.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:14.480
Yet there is something uniquely powerful about&nbsp;
these earlier windows that later artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:19.040
despite their increase in technical&nbsp;
skill, were never able to replicate.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:23.200
Light passing through them&nbsp;
acquires an unusual richness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:29.440
causing the blues and reds to glow&nbsp;
with an almost jewel-like intensity.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:35.440
Later windows rarely produce quite the&nbsp;
same saturation and intensity of color.

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.600
The deep blues and reds of&nbsp;
Chartres possess a visual power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:49.040
that remains one of the cathedral’s most&nbsp;
distinctive and unforgettable qualities.

00:08:54.080 --> 00:09:00.800
The windows are examples of plate tracery,&nbsp;
an early form of Gothic tracery in which the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:07.840
openings are cut directly through a thin stone&nbsp;
wall, and then sculpted into decorative patterns.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:11.920
Chartres reached the height of&nbsp;
sophistication in plate tracery at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:18.240
a time when the newer technique of bar tracery&nbsp;
was beginning to appear elsewhere in France.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:26.320
Chartres owes much to Laon Cathedral.

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.800
The influence is especially evident in the rose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:34.080
windows and in the design of the&nbsp;
north and south transept façades.

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:39.360
These were originally intended to include two&nbsp;
flanking towers that were never completed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:46.480
echoing the composition of Laon's lateral façades.

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:59.920
The projecting triple porches of the transepts&nbsp;
likewise recall the great western front of Laon.

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:07.360
The interior unity of Chartres Cathedral&nbsp;
is not fully reflected on the outside.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:09.200
From a distance across the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:15.520
its silhouette already reveals an unexpected&nbsp;
asymmetry, especially in the two towers.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.640
But it is only when you approach&nbsp;
the west front that this sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:25.120
of imbalance becomes fully apparent.

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.120
Unlike the rest of the building, which&nbsp;
presents a coherent architectural system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:35.120
of light and space, the west&nbsp;
façade appears more fragmented.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:39.920
This has to do with the history of the&nbsp;
building: On the site originally stood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:46.800
an older Romanesque church, later modified with&nbsp;
early Gothic additions in the mid-12th century.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:54.160
Most of this earlier structure was destroyed in a&nbsp;
great fire in 1194, but some parts of it survived,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.160 --> 00:11:00.240
especially the west facade, which consisted&nbsp;
of a central body with three portals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.240 --> 00:11:08.400
three large windows above, a gable on top, and&nbsp;
two lateral towers: the south tower with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:16.400
great spire, which still stands today, and&nbsp;
the north tower, which remained incomplete.

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:23.120
After the fire, the surviving facade was&nbsp;
incorporated into the new construction.

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:29.440
Some of the earlier stained glass, including the&nbsp;
west façade lancets, was preserved and reused.

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:37.840
The central gable was replaced by a large rose&nbsp;
window and crowned with the gallery of kings.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:42.640
A horizontal string course marks the&nbsp;
junction between the old and new work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:50.320
from which the rose window&nbsp;
sits slightly off-center.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:57.680
The south tower was left untouched and retains a&nbsp;
structural clarity with minimal decoration, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:04.480
the north tower was completed centuries later, in&nbsp;
1513, in the Flamboyant Gothic style of the late&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.480 --> 00:12:12.400
Middle Ages, characterized by a more slender,&nbsp;
dematerialized and highly ornamental design.

00:12:12.400 --> 00:12:16.320
One of the most intriguing features&nbsp;
of the cathedral is the relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.320 --> 00:12:22.720
between the great west rose window and the&nbsp;
labyrinth embedded in the floor of the nave.

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:27.520
If you project&nbsp;the elevation of the facade&nbsp;
onto the ground, the center of the rose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.960
window—where Christ is represented—aligns&nbsp;
with the center of the labyrinth below.

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:37.200
The labyrinth represents a&nbsp;
symbolic journey of the soul.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:42.240
Although its path twists and turns through&nbsp;
a long and seemingly complex route,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:47.760
there is ultimately only one course to&nbsp;
follow, and it always leads to the center.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:49.280
For medieval pilgrims,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:55.920
it may have served as a powerful image&nbsp;
of the spiritual journey toward Christ.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:02.480
Despite its asymmetry and apparent fragmentation,&nbsp;
the west facade of Chartres introduced several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.480 --> 00:13:07.920
key elements that would become defining&nbsp;
features of later Gothic architecture.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:16.000
The sculptural program of the portals, the large&nbsp;
central rose window, and the gallery of kings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:22.960
were all innovations that would be not only&nbsp;
included, but also refined and fully integrated as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.960 --> 00:13:31.840
essential components in later facades such as&nbsp;
those of Notre Dame in Paris, and Reims Cathedral.

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:40.080
After the fire, the cathedral was rebuilt in an&nbsp;
exceptionally short period, between 1194 and 1220.

00:13:40.080 --> 00:13:42.720
This was possible thanks to the large number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:47.600
donations received from pilgrims who&nbsp;
came to visit the Tunic of the Virgin.

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:50.320
Because of this rapid construction, most of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.320 --> 00:13:56.960
building reflects a remarkably&nbsp;
coherent architectural vision.

00:13:56.960 --> 00:14:00.720
The structural system of flying&nbsp;
buttresses and the overall logic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:06.960
the design allow for enlarged interior&nbsp;
spaces and greater expanses of glass.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:12.480
In this sense, the interior and exterior&nbsp;
are largely consistent, with the exception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:20.720
of the west façade, which stands apart as the&nbsp;
result of a more complex and layered history.

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:25.200
There are many famous and beautiful&nbsp;
medieval cathedrals in Europe.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:31.600
Many of them evolved over the centuries,&nbsp;
and accumulated Renaissance, Baroque and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:37.760
modern additions, as well as mutilations&nbsp;
from restorations, wars, and revolutions.

00:14:37.760 --> 00:14:41.840
But Chartres Cathedral escaped much of that.

00:14:41.840 --> 00:14:45.920
When you stand in the nave, you're&nbsp;
experiencing something remarkably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.920 --> 00:14:51.520
close to what a visitor around&nbsp;
1220 would have experienced.

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:53.760
That's very rare.

00:14:53.760 --> 00:15:15.200
No Gothic cathedral in Europe preserves the&nbsp;
medieval experience more completely than Chartres.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:17.600
Than you for watching, I&nbsp;
hope you enjoyed and learned.

00:15:17.600 --> 00:15:21.520
Please like this video, and remember that&nbsp;
you can see much more of architecture and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:24.160
cities on my channel, so make sure you subscribe.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:26.800
Thank you for watching, and I'll see&nbsp;
you very soon in another episode.

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:27.760
Goodbye!

